# Line endings are LF in the index AND in every working tree, on every OS.
#
# Without this file, git on Windows checks out with core.autocrlf=true — which
# is the GitHub Actions windows runner's default — and every tracked file
# arrives with CRLF. Two things have broken on that, in the same repo, weeks
# apart:
#
#   - `prettier --check` uses endOfLine: "lf" by default, so the format gate
#     added in this PR reported ALL 524 tracked files as unformatted on
#     windows-2022 while passing on ubuntu. That is what prompted this file.
#   - any test that reads a source file and does a line-oriented scan or an
#     exact string match sees a stray `\r` at every line end. The CSS token
#     and z-index scans under web/src hit this, and the workflow scan added in
#     PR #295 hit it again; each one now normalises defensively at the read.
#
# Those defensive normalisations stay — a test should not assume its input —
# but this is the root cause, and fixing it here means the next scan written
# without that thought does not fail on one runner only.
#
# `text=auto` still lets git detect binaries and leave them alone. Every
# tracked file is already LF in the index, so this changes no stored content;
# it only stops the checkout-time conversion.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The one deliberate exception: the PowerShell installer keeps CRLF on
# checkout, which is what Windows users get today. Nothing formats or scans
# it, so it costs nothing to leave it alone.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
